GX: stages that name an ungenerated texture coordinate; new pipelines saved once built

Issue #39 (SwitchWaker): an ice arrow on a Mothula crashed the game, and from then on every start
crashed in the "Preparing shaders" screen at the same count, also after reinstalling. The frozen
Mothula draws with two texgens (GXSetNumTexGens(2)) and a TEV stage reading GX_TEXCOORD2. The
hardware then reads texture coordinate 0 (Dolphin, PixelShaderGen.cpp: "tex coord 0 is used");
Aurora's shader generator found no texgen for coordinate 2 (src GX_MAX_TEXGENSRC) and aborted with
"unhandled tcg src 21" on the pipeline thread. The config had already been written to
pipeline_cache.db when the draw asked for it, so every later start's warm-up built it again and
aborted before the game started. Reinstalling keeps native/user/cache/.

lib/gx/shader_info.cpp, gx.cpp:
- resolve_ungenerated_texcoords points TEV and indirect stages whose texture coordinate has no
  texgen at GX_TEXCOORD0, as the hardware does (nothing changes when there is no texgen at all).
  populate_pipeline_config calls it, so the shader info, the uniforms and the shader agree.

lib/gfx/pipeline_cache.cpp:
- GX rows read from the cache file get the same resolution, so a cache that already holds such a
  row (the reporter's) builds it instead of aborting: the game starts again without deleting it.
- a GX pipeline first seen in a run is written to the cache file once its build has returned
  (g_unsavedPipelines, persist_built_pipeline), no longer when a draw first asks for it: a
  pipeline whose build crashes the process crashes that run only, not every later start. A clean
  shutdown writes the ones still waiting for their build, and so does
  aurora_save_unbuilt_pipelines (include/aurora/pipeline_cache_save.h) for a clean exit that
  skips the shutdown (SwitchWaker's harness ends with _Exit): without it a pipeline first used
  just before the exit never reached the file, and the next run found it cold again. Clear and
  RmlUi pipelines (no GX shader) are written as before.

